content.text:
I stopped before code because the required Canon gate is unavailable in this session: the local Claude CLI is installed, but not authenticated (`Not logged in · Please run /login`).

I did create an isolated worktree and drafted the new foundation contract here:

[SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-FOUNDATION-007.json](/private/tmp/callisto-release-execution-foundation/.claude/specs/SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-FOUNDATION-007.json)

It resolves the model/graph cycle and defines:

- exact ecosystem-qualified release-operation identity;
- immutable intent, graph-only validation capability, execution state, final receipt, and reconciliation;
- separate raw-artifact and semantic-input digests;
- no registry endpoints/secrets in durable intent;
- sequential receipt persistence and mutation safety.

During self-gating, I found and corrected a real contradiction in the first draft: a complete receipt cannot represent interruption. The spec now separates `ReleaseExecutionStateV1` with `Pending` from a terminal-only `ReleaseReceiptV1`.

I have not committed the draft or started Rust changes, because that would bypass your required `canon:gate-spec → vector → lambda` sequence. Once Claude is authenticated in this worktree—or you have Claude gate this draft and provide its verdict—I can immediately amend blockers, commit the accepted spec, create the challenged plan, and start the TDD implementation batches.

